\code{"base_cols"} attribute that \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}} attaches to
\code{analysis_data}. If \code{analysis_data} has no \code{"base_cols"} attribute (e.g.
it was not built with \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, or the attribute was
dropped by an intervening subsetting operation), the function stops with
an informative error.

The \code{permanova_blocks} argument controls which trait sets are analyzed:

\itemize{
\item \code{"all"}: runs the analysis on each individual block in \code{base_cols}
(typically \code{"veg"}, \code{"flo"}, \code{"fru"}), as well as on every pairwise and
full combination of these blocks (\code{"vegflo"}, \code{"vegfru"}, \code{"flofru"},
\code{"vegflofru"});
\item a character vector naming one or more blocks in \code{base_cols}: runs
the analysis only on the requested block(s), individually.
}

For each trait block, rows with missing values in the relevant columns are
removed via \code{\link[stats:na.omit]{stats::na.omit()}}, and traits with zero variance are dropped.
The grouping variable is the \code{taxon} column of \code{analysis_data}; any taxon
with fewer than \code{min_n} specimens (after the above filtering) is removed
from the block entirely, with a message identifying the removed taxa.
Blocks left with fewer than 2 taxa, fewer than 2 traits, or fewer
specimens than taxa, are skipped with a warning.

\strong{Distance.} The distance between specimens is set by \code{dist_method},
passed to \code{\link[vegan:vegdist]{vegan::vegdist()}}: \code{"gower"} (default), \code{"euclidean"} or
\code{"bray"}. For \code{"euclidean"}, traits are centred and scaled first, so the
result matches the distance underlying \code{\link[=morph_pca]{morph_pca()}}. For \code{"gower"} and
\code{"bray"}, traits are used on their original scale. \code{"bray"} requires
non-negative trait values and stops with an informative error otherwise.

\strong{PERMANOVA.} \code{\link[vegan:adonis2]{vegan::adonis2()}} partitions the distance matrix by
\code{taxon}, with \code{permutations} permutations, testing whether taxa differ in
multivariate location (centroid). The reported statistics are the pseudo-F
ratio, R\eqn{^2} (proportion of the total sum of squares explained by
taxon) and a permutation p-value.

\strong{PERMDISP.} \code{\link[vegan:betadisper]{vegan::betadisper()}} (\code{type = "median"}) computes each
specimen's distance to its taxon's spatial median in the ordination of the
distance matrix, and \code{\link[vegan:permutest.betadisper]{vegan::permutest.betadisper()}} tests whether these
distances differ among taxa, with \code{permutations} permutations. PERMDISP is
the multivariate analogue of a test for homogeneity of variance: a
significant PERMANOVA together with a significant PERMDISP means that the
taxa may differ in dispersion as well as (or instead of) location, so the
PERMANOVA result alone should not be read as evidence of separate
centroids.

\strong{Pairwise tests.} For every pair of taxa retained in a block,
PERMANOVA is repeated on the corresponding sub-matrix of the distance
matrix, and PERMDISP pairwise comparisons are obtained from
\code{\link[vegan:permutest.betadisper]{vegan::permutest.betadisper()}} (\code{pairwise = TRUE}). In both cases,
p-values are adjusted across all pairs within a block with
\code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} (\code{method = p_adjust}).

The random seed (\code{seed}) is set once per block, before the PERMANOVA,
PERMDISP, and all pairwise tests of that block, so permutation p-values do
not depend on the order in which blocks are analyzed.

Taxon colours are taken from the \code{"taxon_colors"} attribute attached to
\code{analysis_data} by \code{\link[=morph_matrix_setting]{morph_matrix_setting()}}, so that each taxon keeps the
same colour used in other plots (e.g. \code{\link[=morph_pca]{morph_pca()}}, \code{\link[=morph_pcoa]{morph_pcoa()}},
\code{\link[=morph_dapc]{morph_dapc()}}). If this attribute is missing, or lacks an entry for a
taxon retained in a given block, colours are generated automatically from
the \code{viridis} palette for the affected taxa, with a warning.

For each analyzed block, the following figures are generated:

\itemize{
\item a boxplot (with individual points) of each specimen's distance to
its taxon's centroid, one box per taxon;
\item a PCoA ordination (axes 1 and 2 of the distance matrix used for
PERMDISP) with a normal confidence ellipse per taxon at level
\code{ellipse_level}.
}

